editeng: AutoCorrect accepts plain language codes without loading liblangtag at start.

The first Writer document of a session creates the AutoCorrect configuration (SwDLL, InitCore). Its
constructor lists the acor_*.dat files and checks each language-only tag with LanguageTag::isValidBcp47,
which initializes liblangtag and loads its language subtag registry: about 40 MB in 255,000 allocations,
held for the whole session, before any document is open (heaptrack of an empty Writer: 39.9 MB of
63 MB). With the shipped files exactly one tag takes that path, a plain language code.

HstIsPlainLanguageCode (new hstlanguagecode.cxx) answers for two or three lowercase letters outside
the private-use range qaa-qtz, which are valid BCP 47 tags without private use; only other tags still
go to liblangtag. The list of AutoCorrect languages is unchanged.

Hook: editeng/source/misc/acorrcfg.cxx (1 condition), editeng/Library_editeng.mk; logic in
editeng/inc/hstlanguagecode.hxx, editeng/source/misc/hstlanguagecode.cxx.
Test: CppunitTest_editeng_core HstLanguageCodeTest (same answer as isValidBcp47 for the shipped codes).
